WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.084 --> 00:00:06.545
Chris Anderson: Our first speaker
was supposed to be

00:00:06.586 --> 00:00:10.173
the amazing creative filmmaker
from LA, Zach King.

00:00:10.215 --> 00:00:13.927
But unfortunately, he's gone missing.

00:00:13.969 --> 00:00:16.805
We've been trying to track him down.

00:00:16.846 --> 00:00:18.807
(Ringing)

00:00:18.848 --> 00:00:19.975
Ah!

00:00:21.476 --> 00:00:24.062
Zach King: Hey, I’m so sorry
I’m not at the conference.

00:00:24.062 --> 00:00:26.481
I'm not going to make it,
I'm stuck at the airport.

00:00:26.481 --> 00:00:29.693
They wouldn't let me in with this,
apparently, it's oversized.

00:00:29.693 --> 00:00:31.111
Wait, you’re onstage.

00:00:31.111 --> 00:00:32.529
Hold on, I have an idea.

00:00:33.238 --> 00:00:35.031
If I just set the phone down here.

00:00:35.615 --> 00:00:36.783
OK.

00:00:37.117 --> 00:00:38.243
This might ...

00:00:41.079 --> 00:00:42.247
Oh.

00:00:42.289 --> 00:00:43.415
(Laughter)

00:00:43.456 --> 00:00:44.624
Hey.

00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:46.209
Sorry I'm late.

00:00:46.209 --> 00:00:47.836
CA: People of TED, Zach King.

00:00:47.877 --> 00:00:51.631
(Applause and cheers)

00:00:51.673 --> 00:00:52.882
ZK: I'm glad that worked

00:00:52.882 --> 00:00:54.926
because sometimes
I get stuck in the screen

00:00:54.926 --> 00:00:56.469
and it becomes a whole thing.

00:00:57.470 --> 00:00:59.764
I have a question for you this morning.

00:00:59.806 --> 00:01:00.932
What is this?

00:01:01.891 --> 00:01:03.685
You can have -- a box, yeah.

00:01:03.727 --> 00:01:05.478
I ask a lot of people that question,

00:01:05.478 --> 00:01:07.230
and most people say it's a box.

00:01:07.272 --> 00:01:12.402
But I've met a small group of people
who have told me that this is not a box.

00:01:12.819 --> 00:01:15.322
And what's fascinating
about this group of people

00:01:15.322 --> 00:01:17.907
is they're the ones
that have pioneered space travel,

00:01:17.949 --> 00:01:19.909
explored the depths of our oceans

00:01:19.909 --> 00:01:22.621
and created some of the most
impenetrable fortresses,

00:01:22.662 --> 00:01:24.456
all before the age of five.

00:01:24.831 --> 00:01:26.708
Of course, I'm talking about my kids.

00:01:26.750 --> 00:01:28.668
See, when I showed them this,

00:01:28.710 --> 00:01:30.962
they told me that this was not a box,

00:01:31.004 --> 00:01:32.756
but rather a rocket ship,

00:01:32.756 --> 00:01:36.217
a submarine and a castle all at once,
somehow combined.

00:01:36.676 --> 00:01:39.721
So why is it that you and I, as adults,

00:01:39.763 --> 00:01:43.099
we look at something like this
and we just call it what it is, a box.

00:01:43.600 --> 00:01:47.687
But our children, they can see it
for more possibilities.

00:01:48.438 --> 00:01:50.190
Over the past decade as a filmmaker,

00:01:50.231 --> 00:01:53.151
I've been on a quest to see
with this childlike wonder again.

00:01:53.151 --> 00:01:56.363
So now when I see something
such as a pool table,

00:01:56.404 --> 00:01:59.407
well, I see an entirely
different type of pool.

00:01:59.449 --> 00:02:01.534
(Laughter)

00:02:01.576 --> 00:02:05.497
And I no longer see a little
soccer ball here.

00:02:05.830 --> 00:02:09.000
It's actually got some life to it,
it's a little Dalmatian puppy.

00:02:10.543 --> 00:02:13.296
I no longer see the glass as half empty.

00:02:13.838 --> 00:02:15.840
In fact, I don't even see a glass at all.

00:02:15.840 --> 00:02:19.177
For me, it's actually a tasty
piece of chocolate cake.

00:02:19.177 --> 00:02:21.429
(Murmurs and laughter)

00:02:21.429 --> 00:02:23.973
See, you and I can still access
this childlike wonder.

00:02:24.015 --> 00:02:26.142
We can still see and dream
with this wonder,

00:02:26.142 --> 00:02:29.229
although it may have been a while
since we've experienced it.

00:02:29.229 --> 00:02:31.398
So today what I want to do for you

00:02:31.439 --> 00:02:33.733
is have you regain
your sense of childlike wonder

00:02:33.775 --> 00:02:35.151
and show you how to do that.

00:02:35.151 --> 00:02:36.319
So, if you will,

00:02:36.319 --> 00:02:37.987
I want to tell you a story,

00:02:38.029 --> 00:02:40.323
and I invite you to imagine with me

00:02:40.323 --> 00:02:43.201
that one Christmas,
a box just like this is wrapped

00:02:43.243 --> 00:02:44.577
and placed under a tree.

00:02:45.245 --> 00:02:47.831
And an excited little boy
comes running down the stairs

00:02:47.831 --> 00:02:48.998
on Christmas morning

00:02:48.998 --> 00:02:52.877
and he unwraps the box
and inside is a bike.

00:02:53.878 --> 00:02:57.173
And although he rides the bike
for a few moments around the room,

00:02:57.215 --> 00:02:59.634
he ends up playing with the box instead.

00:03:00.009 --> 00:03:03.471
Because to him it's a deep, dark cave.

00:03:03.972 --> 00:03:05.140
Hello.

00:03:05.140 --> 00:03:06.182
(Echoes)

00:03:06.224 --> 00:03:08.560
And at the bottom of that cave

00:03:08.601 --> 00:03:10.478
is an X marks the spot.

00:03:10.520 --> 00:03:12.897
And of course, he's the best
pirate on the seas,

00:03:12.939 --> 00:03:15.316
so he's going to dig for buried treasure.

00:03:15.650 --> 00:03:16.818
(Panting)

00:03:16.860 --> 00:03:19.487
It's a lot of work digging for treasure.

00:03:19.487 --> 00:03:20.739
(Metallic sound)

00:03:20.780 --> 00:03:22.490
And that's when he discovered ...

00:03:24.617 --> 00:03:26.703
That he -- (Screams)

00:03:26.745 --> 00:03:27.912
Whoa.

00:03:27.954 --> 00:03:30.999
That he was not the first pirate
to look for this treasure.

00:03:31.291 --> 00:03:34.753
But that boy had many adventures
with this box over the years.

00:03:34.794 --> 00:03:36.838
Once, it was his time machine

00:03:36.880 --> 00:03:39.841
that took him back
to the age of the dinosaurs.

00:03:39.883 --> 00:03:41.009
(Dinosaur screeching)

00:03:41.050 --> 00:03:42.218
Oh!

00:03:42.260 --> 00:03:46.347
Another time it was actually his bunker
to hide from the zombie apocalypse.

00:03:46.347 --> 00:03:48.600
(Screaming)

00:03:49.100 --> 00:03:51.770
And once he even dreamed
that it was a submarine

00:03:51.811 --> 00:03:53.938
that took him to the depths of the ocean

00:03:53.980 --> 00:03:56.024
until he could no longer hold his breath.

00:03:56.065 --> 00:03:58.234
(Water splashing)

00:03:58.818 --> 00:04:00.987
But one day, the boy
couldn't find the box.

00:04:01.029 --> 00:04:02.405
He looked in his room.

00:04:02.405 --> 00:04:04.115
He searched the backyard.

00:04:04.491 --> 00:04:06.284
And then he found it in the garage.

00:04:06.284 --> 00:04:07.994
And when he ran to go play with it,

00:04:07.994 --> 00:04:09.287
he opened it up.

00:04:09.829 --> 00:04:12.207
And inside were winter jackets.

00:04:13.416 --> 00:04:15.418
And this was the day that the boy learned

00:04:15.418 --> 00:04:17.629
what the box was actually made to do.

00:04:18.004 --> 00:04:19.297
To store things.

00:04:19.714 --> 00:04:21.633
Now the box is just a regular box.

00:04:22.133 --> 00:04:26.763
The boy ended up packing up his things
to go to college in that box

00:04:26.805 --> 00:04:28.264
a couple of years later.

00:04:28.306 --> 00:04:29.891
Soon after that, he used that box

00:04:29.891 --> 00:04:32.060
to move to his first home
when he got married.

00:04:32.101 --> 00:04:34.312
And then after that,
to make room for the baby,

00:04:34.354 --> 00:04:36.439
he packed some
of his film gear in this box,

00:04:36.439 --> 00:04:38.399
placed it in the top shelf of his garage,

00:04:38.399 --> 00:04:40.360
where it sat for a while collecting dust.

00:04:40.401 --> 00:04:41.569
See, I was that boy

00:04:41.569 --> 00:04:45.281
and I'm embarrassed to admit that that box
sat on the top shelf of the garage

00:04:45.323 --> 00:04:48.284
for many more years after that,
not being dreamed with.

00:04:49.160 --> 00:04:51.996
So, one night I heard a sound.

00:04:52.038 --> 00:04:53.206
(Crashing)

00:04:53.540 --> 00:04:56.751
It was coming from the garage
and I thought someone was breaking in,

00:04:56.751 --> 00:04:58.169
so I went to go check it out.

00:04:58.211 --> 00:05:00.380
To my surprise, the box was on the ground,

00:05:00.380 --> 00:05:02.465
and my kids were dreaming with the box.

00:05:02.507 --> 00:05:03.675
(Kids laugh)

00:05:03.716 --> 00:05:06.010
And they said, “Daddy,
we’re playing rocket ship.

00:05:06.010 --> 00:05:07.428
Come play with us.”

00:05:07.470 --> 00:05:09.806
It had been a while
since I played with the box,

00:05:09.806 --> 00:05:11.599
but I fired on the engine for them.

00:05:11.641 --> 00:05:12.809
Sure enough,

00:05:12.851 --> 00:05:16.229
after all these years, she still ran
and we headed into space.

00:05:16.729 --> 00:05:18.815
Houston, we're ready for liftoff.

00:05:18.857 --> 00:05:21.776
(Rocket lifts off)

00:05:21.818 --> 00:05:25.238
We ended up going
just a little past Jupiter,

00:05:25.238 --> 00:05:28.741
where we met some friendly space aliens
who taught us a really cool sport

00:05:28.741 --> 00:05:31.202
that I want to show you,
called space soccer.

00:05:34.247 --> 00:05:37.625
It's a really slow sport,
requires a lot of yoga.

00:05:37.625 --> 00:05:39.836
(Laughter)

00:05:39.878 --> 00:05:41.337
But it is more fun than golf.

00:05:42.171 --> 00:05:44.048
So.

00:05:44.048 --> 00:05:45.675
It was there in that garage

00:05:45.717 --> 00:05:49.679
that I learned the key to why all our kids
see with childlike wonder.

00:05:50.471 --> 00:05:52.432
It's because they don't have assumptions.

00:05:52.473 --> 00:05:54.642
They don't have assumptions
about the box yet.

00:05:54.642 --> 00:05:56.853
They haven't been told
what it's supposed to do

00:05:56.895 --> 00:05:58.688
or what it's supposed to be used for.

00:05:58.688 --> 00:06:01.149
Therefore, they have
all these other possibilities.

00:06:01.566 --> 00:06:03.318
So for you and I,

00:06:03.318 --> 00:06:06.487
as we approach life
and try to solve new problems,

00:06:06.529 --> 00:06:09.198
if we're able to remove
our assumptions for a moment,

00:06:09.198 --> 00:06:12.535
we can see with this new childlike
wonder once again.

00:06:12.869 --> 00:06:15.204
So I want to test this out
and see if this worked,

00:06:15.246 --> 00:06:17.999
if this experience regained
a little sense of your wonder.

00:06:17.999 --> 00:06:20.585
So I’m going to throw up
my desktop image on the screen

00:06:20.585 --> 00:06:22.712
and I want you to hold back
your assumptions.

00:06:22.712 --> 00:06:24.964
So on the desktop screen,

00:06:25.006 --> 00:06:27.967
you might have a few assumptions
that you can't hold back,

00:06:27.967 --> 00:06:30.720
you're looking at it going,
I'm really disorganized,

00:06:30.762 --> 00:06:33.598
or you’re assuming that I’ve got
too many projects going on,

00:06:33.640 --> 00:06:35.433
and both of those are true.

00:06:35.475 --> 00:06:39.020
But I want us to look a little deeper
to see the possibility.

00:06:39.312 --> 00:06:42.690
So let me help clear the way here.

00:06:44.651 --> 00:06:49.822
(Leaf blower)

00:06:52.909 --> 00:06:55.036
I was actually looking at the background.

00:06:55.036 --> 00:06:56.621
Maybe I need a vacation.

00:06:56.663 --> 00:06:58.831
You know, it's actually super nice here.

00:07:00.875 --> 00:07:03.127
Hey, I'll take that.

00:07:06.547 --> 00:07:08.925
So the next time somebody tells you...

00:07:08.925 --> 00:07:12.595
Oh, wait, that doesn't make sense.

00:07:13.262 --> 00:07:14.514
How do I get out of here?

00:07:15.723 --> 00:07:17.225
OK, I can figure this out.

00:07:17.266 --> 00:07:19.519
If that's not a box,

00:07:19.560 --> 00:07:21.396
maybe it's something else entirely.

00:07:25.566 --> 00:07:26.776
Maybe ...

00:07:28.528 --> 00:07:29.862
It's a portal.

00:07:29.904 --> 00:07:33.741
(Applause and cheers)

00:07:33.783 --> 00:07:34.909
So the next time ...

00:07:34.951 --> 00:07:37.203
(Applause)

00:07:37.245 --> 00:07:40.164
So the next time somebody tells you
to think outside the box,

00:07:40.206 --> 00:07:41.374
you can tell them,

00:07:41.416 --> 00:07:44.335
"Hey, this is not a box."

00:07:44.669 --> 00:07:46.879
Because when we remove our assumptions,

00:07:46.921 --> 00:07:50.049
we're able to then see
with this childlike sense of wonder.

00:07:50.091 --> 00:07:52.927
And that's the moment
when new ideas can enter the world.

00:07:52.969 --> 00:07:58.349
(Applause and cheers)

